## Phase 1 — Workbook Inventory

Run against the real workbook (engine source in **Appendix A**, save as
`scripts/xl_engine.py`):

```bash
python scripts/xl_engine.py inventory path/to/workbook.xlsm > out/inventory.json
```

The inventory reports, with exact locations:

| Field | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| `sheets` (incl. hidden/veryHidden state) | Hidden sheets frequently hold the real calculation core |
| `defined_names` | The semantic vocabulary of the workbook; graph edges resolve through these |
| `vba_present` | Routes Phase 2; `.xlsm` forces COM-only modification path |
| `external_links`, `external_ref_cells` | Boundary nodes — values frozen at baseline, flagged in every blast radius |
| `calc_settings.iterate` | Iterative calculation ON means intentional circular references exist; diff tolerance policy must widen (see Phase 4) |
| `formula_cells / shared / array` | Scale estimate + parser sanity check |
| `nondeterministic_cells` (`NOW/TODAY/RAND*`) | Mandatory diff exclusion list — without it every regression check false-positives |
| `opaque_ref_cells` (`INDIRECT/OFFSET`) | **Graph blind spots.** Static analysis cannot resolve these references — see the containment downgrade rule in Phase 5 |

**Evidence rule:** if `opaque_ref_cells` is non-empty, the report must list every one and
state: *"Dependency edges through these cells are not statically resolvable. Blast-radius
prediction is advisory for any change reaching these sheets; the diff gate is the
authoritative guard."*

